3. Data Flow Thinking?
  -> Process Chosen: Student Registration
 Step 1 – User Interface (UI)
  ->The student enters personal details such as:
* Name
* Student ID
* Email
* Department
* Phone Number
 Step 2 – Validation
  ->The frontend validates:
* Required fields
* Email format
* Duplicate entries
* Data completeness
If validation fails, an error message is displayed.
 Step 3 – Flow
  ->The validated information is sent from the UI component to the Salesforce server through Apex.
 Step 4 – Apex Processing
  ->The Apex controller:
* Receives student information
* Performs business validations
* Checks system rules
* Prepares records for storage
 Step 5 – Database
  ->The student record is stored in Salesforce objects.
  ->Data is saved securely in the database.
Step 6 – Notification
  ->After successful registration:
* Success message is displayed
* Notification is generated
* Confirmation email can be sent
Modern vs Legacy Thinking
4. Why did Salesforce move from Visualforce and Aura to LWC?
1. Visualforce Limitations
* Older page-centric architecture
* Limited modern JavaScript support
* Lower performance
2. Aura Limitations
* More complex development model
* Slower performance
* Proprietary framework concepts
 Advantages of LWC
* Built on modern web standards
* Better performance
* Faster rendering
* Simpler development experience
* Easier maintenance
* Better scalability
* Improved user experience
Because of these advantages, Salesforce adopted Lightning Web Components as the preferred UI framework.
6. Reflection Why do enterprise applications need modular architecture?
  ->Enterprise applications are large and complex. Modular architecture divides the system into smaller independent components.
Benefits include:
* Easier maintenance
* Better scalability
* Reusable modules
* Faster development
* Improved testing
* Better team collaboration
* Reduced system complexity
Modular architecture allows organizations to grow applications efficiently without affecting existing functionality.

 Revision Questions and Answers
 1. Why do components communicate?
  ->Components communicate to share information, coordinate actions, and create dynamic user experiences within applications.
  ->Communication allows different parts of the application to work together efficiently.
 2. Difference between parent-child communication and events?
 1.Parent-to-Child Communication:
* Uses public properties.
* Parent sends data to child.
 2.Child-to-Parent Communication:
* Uses custom events.
* Child sends information back to parent.
 3. Why is modular architecture useful?
  ->Modular architecture improves maintainability, scalability, testing, and code reusability while reducing complexity.
 4. Why did Salesforce move toward LWC?
  ->Salesforce moved to LWC because it provides better performance, modern web standards support, easier development, and improved user experience.
 5. What problems happen in tightly coupled systems?
  ->Tightly coupled systems are difficult to maintain, test, modify, and scale because changes in one module often affect multiple other modules.
 6. Why is frontend architecture important?
  ->Frontend architecture improves user experience, application performance, maintainability, and component organization.
 7. Why should UI and backend remain separate?
  ->Separating UI and backend improves security, scalability, maintainability, testing, and development efficiency.
 8. Why do large systems need reusable modules?
  ->Reusable modules reduce duplicate code, improve consistency, speed up development, simplify maintenance, and support scalability.
